#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
fire_suppressor - autonomous water-bombing controller for one drone.

Chooses a fire to work, flies to it, gets down onto it and stays there until it
is out, then climbs away and chooses again. The transit half of this is the
reactive laser controller the treasure version of this package used, largely
unchanged and for the same reasons; the ending is different, and the difference
is the scenario.

Algorithm
---------
  TAKEOFF - engage the motors and climb to cruise_altitude. A ground robot is
            always ready to drive; this one has to get airborne first, and
            nothing else can happen until it has.
  ALIGN   - yaw in place until the drone is facing its station.
  CHASE   - fly forward along the current heading at cruise altitude, with
            proportional yaw correction. Speed is scaled down inside slow_dist
            and further reduced by the safety filter near obstacles.
  DESCEND - overhead the fire: stop flying forward, hold the station and come
            down to it. This is where the work happens, and it is held until
            the controller says the fire is out - which for a big fire with no
            crew is longer than one tank of water lasts.
  TOWATER - dry, or nearly: fly to a tender instead of a fire. Same transit
            rules as CHASE, different destination.
  REFILL  - hold station over the tender's deck and take water.
  CLIMB   - back to cruise altitude before going anywhere.
  WAIT    - a couple of ticks after a drop, so the next choice is made from a
            board that already reflects the fire that just went out.

Water, and why running dry is not the same as giving up
-------------------------------------------------------
This aircraft carries about ten seconds of bombing. A fire above about an 8
needs more than that, so running dry mid-job is the normal case rather than a
fault, and the response is a *detour*, not a re-decision: the drone keeps its
claim on the fire, flies to a scout tender, fills up, and comes back to the
same station. Releasing the fire instead would be wrong twice over - it would
strand crewmates who sized the job on this drone being part of it, and it would
put the aircraft back into a market where the fire it just spent a tank on
looks no different from any other.

The controller understands this: its commitment timeout is refreshed by being
*on station*, not by doing useful work, and it is long enough to cover a round
trip to a tender. A drone that is present and dry is waiting for water.

Before claiming anything, though, the drone tops up if it is below
~reserve_litres. Arriving at a fire with two seconds of water in the tank
helps nobody and burns a whole transit doing it.


Choosing a fire
---------------
Nobody hands this drone a job any more. fire_spotter publishes the whole board
on /bushfire/hotspots - every fire's position, value, work remaining and crew -
and this node picks one and says so on /bushfire/claims.

What it maximises is share per second, not value and not distance:

    n      = crew + 1                     (me, joining)
    share  = intensity / n                (paid equally to everyone who chose)
    eta    = travel + work_left / n       (n drones do n seconds per second)
    utility= share / eta

Both halves of a crew matter and they pull opposite ways. Joining halves the
payout, and it also halves the time - so on a small fire joining is pointless,
and on a big one it is the best thing available to everybody, which is how
bigger fires end up with bigger crews without anything in the code saying so.
Distance enters as travel time in the denominator rather than as a rule, so a
nearer fire wins ties and a fire across the arena has to be worth the flight.

Having chosen, the drone is committed: the spotter ignores its claims on
anything else until the fire is out. That rule is not a restriction on this
node so much as the thing that makes its own arithmetic true - an `eta`
computed from a crew of three is a lie if two of them can leave.

The claim is re-sent until a tasking comes back, because a claim can be refused
(the crew was full) and because it can simply be dropped. If nothing comes back
within ~claim_timeout the drone picks again from a fresher board, which handles
both cases with the same code. In the other direction, taskings arriving is
what proves the commitment still exists: if they stop for ~target_timeout the
drone considers itself released - that is how it learns the spotter dropped it
for not turning up.

Where the drone actually stops
------------------------------
Not over the fire, unless it is alone on it. The spotter gives each crew member
a station - a slot on a ring around the fire, at its own altitude - and sends
it in station_x/y/z on every tasking. Two aircraft station-keeping on the same
point is a collision, and with crews that is no longer a coincidence to be
tolerated but the normal case. So CHASE flies at the station, DESCEND holds the
station, and the fire's own x/y is used only for the display and for the
heading the drone is roughly pointed along.

The station moves when the crew changes size. That is handled by station-
keeping already doing what it does - the error simply steps - and it is another
reason DESCEND is a position controller rather than an arrival test.

Why the last three phases exist
-------------------------------
A floating treasure was collected by arriving at a point in the air, so the
chase ended when the drone got there and altitude was just another axis of the
same approach. A fire is on the ground and cannot be flown to - the aircraft
would have to land on it. So arrival and suppression come apart into two
phases: CHASE ends overhead, at cruise, and then the drone has to descend
through the last metre and a half onto a target it is now directly above.

That descent cannot be flown body-forward the way CHASE is, because "forward"
is not a useful direction when the error is 20 cm in an arbitrary bearing and
the drone is not yawing any more. DESCEND rotates the world-frame position
error into the body frame and commands both linear.x and linear.y, which is
proper station-keeping. It is the one place this node uses lateral velocity,
and it is safe to do it here precisely because the drone is stationary over an
assigned fire that the spotter placed clear of the trees - the laser's sectors
stop meaning anything when the aircraft translates sideways, which is why
CHASE never does it.

CLIMB is not decoration either. Leaving at drop altitude means crossing the
fire ground at 1.0 m, under the height the geofence and the avoidance were
tuned for, and into whatever the next fire is next to.

Which frame /cmd_vel is in
--------------------------
The body one, yaw-stabilized. This is worth stating flatly because getting it
wrong is silent and fatal, and this package had it wrong: TwistController's
cmd_vel callback sets

    command_given_in_stabilized_frame_ = true;      // twist_controller.cpp:139

and update() then rotates the command by the current yaw before using it. Only
the command/twist topic - which nothing here publishes - is taken in the world
frame.

So a command of (speed, 0) means "forward", whatever the drone is pointed at.
Building the command as (speed*cos(yaw), speed*sin(yaw)) - which is what a
world-frame interface would want - gets rotated by yaw a second time and flies
the aircraft along 2*yaw. That is not a wobble: at yaw 95 degrees the drone
travels at 190, i.e. backwards and sideways, with its laser pointed at empty
air. Measured that exactly - a drone yawed to 94.7 degrees moving along 215 -
before this was found, and the whole fleet spent every run grinding into walls
it was not looking at and scoring nothing.

Everything this node publishes is therefore body-frame: linear.x is forward,
linear.y is unused, and linear.z is untouched by the transform and so is the
same in either frame.

Why ALIGN exists on an aircraft
-------------------------------
Given the above it is not optional, but the reason is the laser rather than the
frame. The scanner is bolted to the airframe, so its "front" sector only means
"where I am going" if the drone is pointed where it is going. ALIGN is what
keeps those two the same thing; CHASE then flies body-forward and the safety
filter's three sectors mean what their names say.

Geofence and rescue
-------------------
A drone that is upset - clipped a tree, caught another drone's downwash - does
not recover on its own here, and the reason is worth writing down. The launch
sets hector's auto_engage false, and in this scenario that is not optional at
all: auto_engage cuts the motors when it sees a commanded descent that is not
being achieved, which is landing detection, and DESCEND is a commanded descent
onto the ground held for as long as it takes. But twist_controller.cpp nests
its flip-over check *inside* the same auto_engage block:

    if (auto_engage_) {
      ...
      if (motors_running_ && load_factor < 0.0) {   // flip over?
        motors_running_ = false;

so turning the one off turns the other off with it. An inverted aircraft then
keeps full thrust pointing wherever its rotors happen to face and leaves. That
is not a hypothetical: uav_1 clipped a wall, went over it at 3.5 m, and was
still accelerating 275 m away with the motors running when the run ended.

So there are two fences. Outside fence_half_size the drone stops working the
fire and flies back to the middle - an ordinary soft geofence. Past
fence_hard_size, or tilted past upset_angle, it is no longer a control problem,
and the drone is teleported back to its launch point with
/gazebo/set_model_state and re-engaged. Respawning is the honest fix in a
simulator: there is no attitude recovery to write, because the controller that
would fly it is the one that has already lost the aircraft.

A respawn has to cut the motors on the way past, not just move the aircraft.
TwistController only clears its PID integrators in the branch it takes when the
motors are *not* running, so with auto_engage off there is no path that ever
resets them. Teleporting alone leaves the attitude loop wound up against the
tumble it was fighting a moment ago, and it spends that integral on the
freshly-levelled aircraft and puts it straight back over. That is a respawn
loop, and it is self-sustaining: 53 rescues in 90 seconds, none of which flew.
So the order is /shutdown, teleport, settle, /engage - the shutdown is what
makes the reset happen. Rescues are also rate-limited by settle_time, because
a drone that has just been dropped in bounces on its landing gear and the
attitude that produces is not an upset.

What the laser cannot see
-------------------------
It is a horizontal 270-degree plane. It says nothing about what is above or
below the aircraft, so altitude is flown open-loop with respect to obstacles
and only min_altitude keeps it off the ground. That is survivable here because
the trees are vertical and their trunks run the full height the drone flies at,
so anything it could hit, it can also see - which is exactly why the canopies
in worlds/bushfire.world carry no collision geometry. A scene with real
overhanging branches would need a sensor this aircraft does not have.

It also means the descent is blind downwards. Nothing stops the drone flying
into the ground except drop_altitude and min_altitude being honest numbers, so
DESCEND clamps against min_altitude like every other phase rather than
commanding its way to zero.

Self-hits, and why the filter for them is conditional
-----------------------------------------------------
Past about 32 degrees of tilt the scan plane dips through the rotor discs and
the drone sees its own booms at their tip radius, ~0.40 m (the mast height in
urdf/quadrotor_laser_mast.gazebo.xacro is where that number comes from). Read
as obstacles those returns mean "boxed in", and the aircraft stops for good.

The obvious guard - drop everything closer than 0.55 m - is worse than the
disease, because it is precisely the range at which this drone gets into
trouble. Jammed against a tree the laser reads 0.33 m; wedged in a corner,
0.24 m. Both are below the threshold, so both are discarded, and a drone in
contact with a wall sees a clear path through it and holds full forward thrust
against it until it tips over. Two of the three drones ended a run that way.

So the rejection is conditional on the thing that causes it. Tilt is known -
it is in the same odometry the controller already reads - and below
self_hit_tilt the plane is provably above the rotors, so returns are trusted
down to the sensor floor. Above it, returns inside self_hit_range are dropped.
The drone is briefly blind while recovering hard, which is the one moment it is
not steering by the laser anyway.

Subscriptions
-------------
  ground_truth/state      nav_msgs/Odometry          Drone pose + velocity
  bushfire/target         uav_bushfire/Hotspot       This drone's job
  /bushfire/hotspots      uav_bushfire/HotspotArray  The board, to choose from
  scan                    sensor_msgs/LaserScan      Hokuyo, on the mast

Publications
------------
  cmd_vel                 geometry_msgs/Twist        Body-frame velocity (~20 Hz)
  /bushfire/claims        uav_bushfire/FireClaim     The fire it has chosen

Services used
-------------
  engage                  std_srvs/Empty         Spin the motors up
  shutdown                std_srvs/Empty         Cut them, to clear the PIDs

ROS Parameters (private, ~)
---------------------------
  ~cruise_altitude float  Transit altitude of lane 0, m      (default 1.6)
  ~cruise_step     float  Each lane flies this much higher
                          than the last, m                   (default 0.45)
  ~drop_altitude   float  Fallback bombing height if the
                          spotter sends no station, m        (default 0.95)
  ~max_crew        int    Do not claim a fire that already
                          has this many drones on it         (default 3)
  ~join_deadline   float  ...nor one already this far
                          through its work, as a fraction    (default 0.7)
  ~choose_hz       float  Rate the board is re-read at (Hz)  (default 2.0)
  ~claim_timeout   float  Re-pick if a claim goes unanswered
                          for this long, s                   (default 3.0)
  ~target_timeout  float  Consider a commitment released if
                          taskings stop for this long, s     (default 2.0)
  ~min_altitude    float  Never command below this, m        (default 0.7)
  ~linear_speed    float  Max forward speed (m/s)            (default 1.2)
  ~vertical_speed  float  Max climb/descent rate (m/s)       (default 0.6)
  ~station_gain    float  P-gain for holding over a fire     (default 1.2)
  ~station_speed   float  Speed cap while station-keeping    (default 0.6)
  ~angular_gain    float  P-gain for heading error           (default 2.0)
  ~max_angular     float  Yaw rate cap (rad/s)               (default 1.5)
  ~angle_tol       float  Heading tolerance (rad)            (default 0.12)
  ~slow_dist       float  Begin slowing at this dist (m)     (default 1.0)
  ~arrive_radius   float  Switch to DESCEND inside this, m   (default 0.70)
  ~climb_tol       float  Cruise reached within this, m      (default 0.20)
  ~avoid_dist      float  Avoidance activation range (m)     (default 1.6)
  ~stop_dist       float  Full-stop obstacle range (m)       (default 0.9)
  ~min_valid_range float  Sensor floor; drop returns below   (default 0.15)
  ~self_hit_range  float  Drop returns below this when tilted(default 0.50)
  ~self_hit_tilt   float  Tilt at which the mast stops
                          clearing the rotors, rad           (default 0.50)
  ~back_off_speed  float  Reverse speed when fully blocked   (default 0.35)
  ~avoid_hold      float  Stay committed to a deflection for
                          this long after it clears, s       (default 1.5)
  ~fence_half_size float  Soft geofence half-width, m        (default 4.8)
  ~fence_hard_size float  Respawn beyond this half-width, m  (default 8.0)
  ~fence_max_alt   float  Respawn above this altitude, m     (default 4.0)
  ~upset_angle     float  Respawn past this roll/pitch, rad  (default 1.05)
  ~settle_time     float  Ignore upsets for this long after
                          a respawn, s                       (default 1.5)
  ~start_x         float  Launch point, for respawns         (default 0.0)
  ~start_y         float  Launch point, for respawns         (default 0.0)
  ~control_hz      float  Control loop rate (Hz)             (default 20.0)
  ~status_hz       float  Status display rate (Hz)           (default 2.0)
"""

import math

import rospy
from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist
from nav_msgs.msg import Odometry
from sensor_msgs.msg import LaserScan
from std_srvs.srv import Empty
from gazebo_msgs.msg import ModelState
from gazebo_msgs.srv import SetModelState
from tf.transformations import euler_from_quaternion

from uav_bushfire.msg import Hotspot, HotspotArray, FireClaim
from uav_scout_bushfire.msg import WaterBoard

_RESET  = '\033[0m'
_BOLD   = '\033[1m'
_YELLOW = '\033[93m'
_GREEN  = '\033[92m'
_CYAN   = '\033[96m'
_RED    = '\033[91m'
_BLUE   = '\033[94m'
_WHITE  = '\033[97m'


def _bar(value, max_value=10.0, width=10):
    filled = round(width * value / max(max_value, 1))
    return _GREEN + '#' * filled + _RESET + '-' * (width - filled)


def _normalize_angle(a):
    while a > math.pi:
        a -= 2.0 * math.pi
    while a < -math.pi:
        a += 2.0 * math.pi
    return a


def _clamp(v, lo, hi):
    return max(lo, min(hi, v))


class FireSuppressor:

    def __init__(self):
        rospy.init_node('fire_suppressor', anonymous=False)

        self._ns = rospy.get_namespace().strip('/') or 'uav'

        # Each drone gets its own transit lane. Every contact measured in the
        # 20 x 20 arena happened at z = 1.9-2.0 m, which was cruise altitude
        # for all three of them: 8.5% of pair-samples were inside the airframe
        # (worst 0.01 m horizontally, 0.08 m vertically) and every one was a
        # pair of aircraft in ALIGN, CHASE or TOWATER - transit, not tender
        # queueing and not fires. The crew ring and the tender ring separate
        # drones where they *stop*; nothing separated them where they fly, and
        # a bigger arena means far more time flying.
        #
        # The band available is tight and both ends are load-bearing. Below
        # ~1.5 m is the suppression ceiling, and a drone transiting through it
        # would register as bombing whatever it passed over. Above 3.0 m is the
        # top of the trunks, and an aircraft that can fly over a tree never
        # exercises the reactive avoidance this whole package is built around.
        # 0.45 m a lane fits three between 1.85 and 2.75 with margin at both
        # ends, and 0.45 m is more than the airframe is tall, so two drones in
        # different lanes cannot touch however their paths cross.
        base_cruise        = rospy.get_param('~cruise_altitude', 1.6)
        cruise_step        = rospy.get_param('~cruise_step',     0.45)
        self._drop_alt     = rospy.get_param('~drop_altitude',   0.95)
        self._min_alt      = rospy.get_param('~min_altitude',    0.7)
        self._lin_speed    = rospy.get_param('~linear_speed',    1.2)
        self._vert_speed   = rospy.get_param('~vertical_speed',  0.6)
        self._stn_gain     = rospy.get_param('~station_gain',    1.2)
        self._stn_speed    = rospy.get_param('~station_speed',   0.6)
        self._ang_gain     = rospy.get_param('~angular_gain',    2.0)
        self._max_ang      = rospy.get_param('~max_angular',     1.5)
        self._angle_tol    = rospy.get_param('~angle_tol',       0.12)
        self._slow_dist    = rospy.get_param('~slow_dist',       1.0)
        self._arrive_rad   = rospy.get_param('~arrive_radius',   0.70)
        self._climb_tol    = rospy.get_param('~climb_tol',       0.20)
        self._avoid_dist   = rospy.get_param('~avoid_dist',      1.6)
        self._stop_dist    = rospy.get_param('~stop_dist',       0.9)
        self._min_range    = rospy.get_param('~min_valid_range', 0.15)
        self._self_hit     = rospy.get_param('~self_hit_range',  0.50)
        self._self_hit_tilt = rospy.get_param('~self_hit_tilt',  0.50)
        self._back_off     = rospy.get_param('~back_off_speed',  0.35)
        self._avoid_hold   = rospy.get_param('~avoid_hold',      1.5)
        self._fence        = rospy.get_param('~fence_half_size', 4.8)
        self._fence_hard   = rospy.get_param('~fence_hard_size', 8.0)
        self._fence_alt    = rospy.get_param('~fence_max_alt',   4.0)
        self._upset_angle  = rospy.get_param('~upset_angle',     1.05)
        self._settle_time  = rospy.get_param('~settle_time',     1.5)
        self._start_x      = rospy.get_param('~start_x',         0.0)
        self._start_y      = rospy.get_param('~start_y',         0.0)
        self._max_crew     = int(rospy.get_param('~max_crew',      3))
        self._join_deadline = rospy.get_param('~join_deadline',  0.7)
        self._reserve      = rospy.get_param('~reserve_litres',  12.0)
        self._dry          = rospy.get_param('~dry_litres',       0.4)
        self._full_frac    = rospy.get_param('~full_fraction',   0.97)
        self._refill_alt   = rospy.get_param('~refill_altitude',  0.95)
        self._refill_rad   = rospy.get_param('~refill_radius',    0.45)
        self._hold_rad     = rospy.get_param('~hold_radius',      2.00)
        # Peer separation. The bubble is a little over two rotor spans so the
        # push starts before contact, and the vertical gate is under the lane
        # step (0.45) so two aircraft on adjacent lanes never trigger it.
        # Biggest fire first, rather than the most efficient one. See _choose.
        self._value_first = rospy.get_param('~value_first', True)
        self._peer_radius = rospy.get_param('~peer_radius', 1.20)
        self._peer_vsep   = rospy.get_param('~peer_vsep',   0.35)
        self._peer_push   = rospy.get_param('~peer_push',   0.60)
        self._hold_i       = int(rospy.get_param('~hold_index',      0))
        self._hold_n       = int(rospy.get_param('~hold_count',      3))
        self._cruise_alt   = base_cruise + cruise_step * self._hold_i
        self._hold_step    = rospy.get_param('~hold_altitude_step', 0.40)
        self._claim_to     = rospy.get_param('~claim_timeout',   3.0)
        self._target_to    = rospy.get_param('~target_timeout',  2.0)
        choose_hz          = rospy.get_param('~choose_hz',       2.0)
        control_hz         = rospy.get_param('~control_hz',     20.0)
        status_hz          = rospy.get_param('~status_hz',       2.0)

        self._x = self._y = self._z = 0.0
        self._roll = self._pitch = self._yaw = 0.0
        self._odom_ready = False
        self._rescues = 0
        self._settle_until = rospy.Time(0)

        self._scan = None
        self._avoiding = False
        self._avoid_until = rospy.Time(0)
        self._fenced = False

        self._fire = None
        self._state = 'TAKEOFF'
        self._wait_ticks = 0
        self._score = 0.0
        self._drops = 0
        self._history = []

        # The board, and this drone's position in the market for it.
        self._board = []
        self._board_stamp = rospy.Time(0)
        self._pending = None            # fire id claimed but not yet confirmed
        self._pending_since = rospy.Time(0)
        self._last_target = rospy.Time(0)
        self._peer_claims = {}          # other drone -> (fire id, when)

        # Water. `_tender` is the vehicle this drone is currently flying to or
        # sitting over; it is chosen once per excursion rather than every tick,
        # because a tender that stops being the nearest one halfway through the
        # trip is not a reason to turn round.
        self._litres = 0.0
        self._capacity = 1.0
        self._tenders = []
        self._tender = None
        self._resume = None             # state to return to once full

        rospy.Subscriber('ground_truth/state', Odometry, self._odom_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('bushfire/target', Hotspot, self._target_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('/bushfire/hotspots', HotspotArray, self._board_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('/bushfire/claims', FireClaim, self._peer_claim_cb)

        # Where the other aircraft actually are.
        #
        # The laser cannot answer this. It is a horizontal plane on a mast, and
        # the whole point of the transit lanes is that the aircraft fly at
        # different heights - so the scan of the one below passes clean under
        # the one above and each is a hole in the other's picture. They become
        # visible to each other only while crossing lanes, which is exactly the
        # manoeuvre that puts two of them in the same place: a descent onto a
        # fire goes down through every lane beneath it.
        #
        # So separation is done from known positions instead of from the
        # sensor, which is the same reason the tenders are told where the trees
        # are rather than being asked to find them.
        self._peer_pose = {}
        for peer in rospy.get_param('~peers', []):
            if peer == self._ns:
                continue
            rospy.Subscriber('/%s/ground_truth/state' % peer, Odometry,
                             self._peer_pose_cb, peer, queue_size=1)
        rospy.Subscriber('/bushfire/water', WaterBoard, self._water_cb)
        rospy.Subscriber('scan', LaserScan, self._scan_cb)

        self._cmd_pub = rospy.Publisher('cmd_vel', Twist, queue_size=1)
        self._claim_pub = rospy.Publisher('/bushfire/claims', FireClaim,
                                          queue_size=10)

        # Held open rather than looked up per call: a rescue is the one moment
        # this node is in a hurry, and it needs all three of these.
        self._engage_srv = None
        self._shutdown_srv = None
        self._set_state_srv = None
        self._connect_services()

        self._engage()

        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / control_hz), self._control_loop)
        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / choose_hz), self._market_loop)
        rospy.Timer(rospy.Duration(1.0 / status_hz), self._status_display)

        self._print_header()

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ motors ---
    def _connect_services(self):
        try:
            rospy.wait_for_service('engage', timeout=60.0)
            rospy.wait_for_service('shutdown', timeout=10.0)
            rospy.wait_for_service('/gazebo/set_model_state', timeout=10.0)
        except rospy.ROSException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] services never appeared: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return
        self._engage_srv    = rospy.ServiceProxy('engage', Empty)
        self._shutdown_srv  = rospy.ServiceProxy('shutdown', Empty)
        self._set_state_srv = rospy.ServiceProxy('/gazebo/set_model_state',
                                                 SetModelState)

    def _engage(self):
        """Spin the motors up.

        The launch turns hector's auto_engage off. Left on it engages on the
        first commanded climb and, more to the point, cuts the motors when it
        sees a commanded descent it cannot achieve. That is landing detection,
        and DESCEND is indistinguishable from a landing by design: a commanded
        descent onto the ground, held at drop_altitude for as long as it takes
        the spotter to register the drop.
        """
        if self._engage_srv is None:
            return
        try:
            self._engage_srv()
            rospy.loginfo('[%s] motors engaged', self._ns)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] could not engage motors: %s', self._ns, exc)

    def _shutdown_motors(self):
        """Cut the motors, which is the only thing that resets the PIDs.

        See "Geofence and rescue" above: with auto_engage off, TwistController
        never takes the branch that calls reset(), so this is the sole way to
        empty its integrators.
        """
        if self._shutdown_srv is None:
            return
        try:
            self._shutdown_srv()
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logwarn('[%s] could not shut motors down: %s', self._ns, exc)

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- fence ---
    def _lost(self):
        """True if the aircraft is beyond saving by ordinary control."""
        return (abs(self._x) > self._fence_hard or
                abs(self._y) > self._fence_hard or
                self._z > self._fence_alt or
                abs(self._roll) > self._upset_angle or
                abs(self._pitch) > self._upset_angle)

    def _outside_fence(self):
        return abs(self._x) > self._fence or abs(self._y) > self._fence

    def _rescue(self):
        """Teleport back to the launch point, stopped and level, and re-engage.

        Order matters. The motors go off *first*: that is what empties the
        attitude PIDs, and a teleport without it hands the freshly-levelled
        aircraft the integral the controller had wound up fighting the tumble.
        """
        self._rescues += 1
        rospy.logwarn('[%s] RESCUE #%d - lost at (%.1f, %.1f, %.1f) '
                      'roll=%.0f pitch=%.0f deg; respawning at (%.1f, %.1f)',
                      self._ns, self._rescues, self._x, self._y, self._z,
                      math.degrees(self._roll), math.degrees(self._pitch),
                      self._start_x, self._start_y)

        self._cmd_pub.publish(Twist())
        self._shutdown_motors()

        # Give the fire up on the way past. The commitment rule exists so that
        # a crew can count on its members, and an aircraft about to be
        # teleported to its launch point is exactly the case where they cannot:
        # it would keep its share while contributing nothing and hold a station
        # its crewmates could have used. Releasing is the honest version of the
        # spotter's timeout, taken by the node that already knows.
        self._release_claim('rescued')

        state = ModelState()
        state.model_name = self._ns
        state.reference_frame = 'world'
        state.pose.position.x = self._start_x
        state.pose.position.y = self._start_y
        state.pose.position.z = 0.3
        state.pose.orientation.w = 1.0
        if self._set_state_srv is None:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] respawn unavailable - no set_model_state',
                         self._ns)
            return
        try:
            self._set_state_srv(state)
        except rospy.ServiceException as exc:
            rospy.logerr('[%s] respawn failed: %s', self._ns, exc)
            return

        # Let the teleport settle before commanding anything, or the first
        # command is computed against the pose it had before it moved. The
        # motors are off through this, so nothing is being flown.
        rospy.sleep(0.5)
        self._cmd_pub.publish(Twist())
        self._engage()
        self._state = 'TAKEOFF'
        self._avoiding = False

        # Landing gear bounce is not an upset. Without this hold the next tick
        # sees the attitude of a drone still settling on its feet and rescues
        # it again, which is how a single bad moment became 53 of them.
        self._settle_until = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(self._settle_time)

    # ------------------------------------------------------ laser helpers ---
    def _sector_min(self, deg_from, deg_to):
        """Minimum valid range in an angular sector, degrees, 0 = forward.

        The scan is 270 degrees centred on forward, not the TurtleBot3's full
        360, so index-to-angle uses angle_min and angle_increment from the
        message rather than tb3_treasure's `i * 360 / n`. That shortcut is only
        correct for a scanner that closes the circle, and using it here would
        silently rotate every sector by 45 degrees.

        The floor is normally just the sensor's own, so a wall the drone is
        already touching is still a wall. It rises to self_hit_range only while
        the aircraft is tilted past the angle at which the mast stops clearing
        the rotors - see "Self-hits" at the top of this file.
        """
        scan = self._scan
        if scan is None:
            return float('inf')

        floor = max(scan.range_min, self._min_range)
        if max(abs(self._roll), abs(self._pitch)) > self._self_hit_tilt:
            floor = max(floor, self._self_hit)
        a = deg_from % 360.0
        b = deg_to % 360.0
        best = float('inf')
        for i, r in enumerate(scan.ranges):
            if not math.isfinite(r) or not (floor < r < scan.range_max):
                continue
            deg = math.degrees(scan.angle_min + i * scan.angle_increment) % 360.0
            in_sector = (a <= deg <= b) if a <= b else (deg >= a or deg <= b)
            if in_sector and r < best:
                best = r
        return best

    def _peer_filter(self, cmd):
        """Push away from another aircraft that is too close.

        Only when the two overlap **vertically**. Three drones on three lanes
        pass over each other constantly and by design; treating that as a
        conflict would have every transit fighting the aircraft above it, and
        the lanes exist precisely so those crossings are free. What is not free
        is two aircraft at the same height within a rotor span, which happens
        when one is descending through another's lane or when two converge on
        the same fire.

        The push is added to the body-frame command rather than replacing it,
        so the drone keeps doing its job while being nudged clear. It is capped
        at ~peer_push so it can never overpower the station-keeping and throw an
        aircraft off a fire it is bombing.
        """
        if not self._peer_pose:
            return cmd
        px = py = 0.0
        for _, (ox, oy, oz) in self._peer_pose.items():
            if abs(self._z - oz) > self._peer_vsep:
                continue                      # cleanly stacked, no conflict
            dx, dy = self._x - ox, self._y - oy
            d = math.hypot(dx, dy)
            if d >= self._peer_radius or d < 1e-3:
                continue
            # Linear ramp: nothing at the edge of the bubble, full push at
            # contact. Summed over peers so the middle aircraft of three is
            # pushed out of the pair rather than towards one of them.
            w = (self._peer_radius - d) / self._peer_radius
            px += (dx / d) * w
            py += (dy / d) * w

        if px == 0.0 and py == 0.0:
            return cmd

        mag = math.hypot(px, py)
        if mag > 1.0:
            px, py = px / mag, py / mag
        c, s_ = math.cos(self._yaw), math.sin(self._yaw)
        bx = px * c + py * s_
        by = -px * s_ + py * c
        cmd.linear.x = _clamp(cmd.linear.x + self._peer_push * bx,
                              -self._lin_speed, self._lin_speed)
        cmd.linear.y = _clamp(cmd.linear.y + self._peer_push * by,
                              -self._lin_speed, self._lin_speed)
        return cmd

    def _lateral_filter(self, cmd):
        """Stop the aircraft strafing into something it can see.

        The three-sector filter below shapes forward speed and yaw and nothing
        else, which was fine while it only ran on CHASE. But DESCEND and the
        station-keeping over a fire command **linear.y**, and a sideways
        command is invisible to a filter that only scales linear.x. A drone
        holding station beside a trunk drifts laterally into it with the
        avoidance engaged the whole time and structurally unable to help.

        Measured before this existed: every drone-tree contact in a run was a
        graze at 0.00 to 0.17 m while the aircraft was at station altitude, not
        a head-on strike in transit.

        So lateral commands are damped against the side sectors, on the same
        taper the forward channel uses. +y is left in the body frame.

        Deliberately outside the front-sector early return: an aircraft beside
        a trunk has nothing in front of it, so the branch below would return
        untouched exactly when this is needed.
        """
        left = self._sector_min(10.0, 80.0)
        right = self._sector_min(280.0, 350.0)
        span = max(self._avoid_dist - self._stop_dist, 1e-3)

        if cmd.linear.y > 0.0 and left < self._avoid_dist:
            ratio = _clamp((left - self._stop_dist) / span, 0.0, 1.0)
            cmd.linear.y *= ratio
        elif cmd.linear.y < 0.0 and right < self._avoid_dist:
            ratio = _clamp((right - self._stop_dist) / span, 0.0, 1.0)
            cmd.linear.y *= ratio
        return cmd

    def _safety_filter(self, cmd):
        """Reactive obstacle avoidance, applied to CHASE commands.

        Same three-sector logic as tb3_treasure - front +-45, left 10-80,
        right 280-350 - and, like tb3_treasure's, it works directly on
        cmd.linear.x, because /cmd_vel here is body-frame too. Vertical
        velocity is left alone: the laser has nothing to say about it.

        Lateral velocity is handled by _lateral_filter, which runs first and
        unconditionally - see there for why it cannot live inside the front
        sector's early return.
        """
        cmd = self._peer_filter(self._lateral_filter(cmd))
        front = self._sector_min(315.0, 45.0)

        if front > self._avoid_dist:
            self._avoiding = False
            return cmd

        self._avoiding = True
        self._avoid_until = rospy.Time.now() + rospy.Duration(self._avoid_hold)
        left = self._sector_min(10.0, 80.0)
        right = self._sector_min(280.0, 350.0)
        turn_left = left >= right

        if front <= self._stop_dist:
            # Stop, back away, and yaw toward the clearer side.
            #
            # tb3_treasure only stops and spins, which works for a robot that
            # can pivot on the spot and then drive out along a new heading.
            # This one cannot always: caught against a wall it spins, and every
            # heading it turns to still has the wall in front, so it spins for
            # the rest of the run. Observed exactly that - all three drones
            # pinned to walls at max yaw rate, scoring nothing. Reversing is
            # what actually breaks the deadlock, and it is free here because a
            # multirotor has no preferred direction of travel.
            cmd.linear.x = -self._back_off
            cmd.linear.y = 0.0
            cmd.angular.z = self._max_ang if turn_left else -self._max_ang
        else:
            ratio = ((front - self._stop_dist) /
                     (self._avoid_dist - self._stop_dist))
            cmd.linear.x *= ratio

            avoid_w = self._max_ang * (1.0 - ratio)
            if turn_left:
                cmd.angular.z = min(self._max_ang, cmd.angular.z + avoid_w)
            else:
                cmd.angular.z = max(-self._max_ang, cmd.angular.z - avoid_w)

        return cmd

    # ------------------------------------------------------------ market ---
    def _release_claim(self, why):
        """Tell the spotter this drone is no longer on its fire."""
        fire_id = (self._fire.id if self._fire is not None else self._pending)
        if fire_id is None:
            return
        claim = FireClaim()
        claim.drone = self._ns
        claim.fire_id = fire_id
        claim.release = True
        self._claim_pub.publish(claim)
        rospy.logwarn('[%s] releasing fire %d - %s', self._ns, fire_id, why)
        self._fire = None
        self._pending = None

    def _station(self):
        """Where to fly to: this drone's slot, not the fire itself.

        The spotter owns the geometry, because it is the only node that knows
        how many drones are on the fire and in what order they joined. A zeroed
        station - an older spotter, or a message built by hand - falls back to
        the fire, which is also what a lone drone's station is anyway.
        """
        f = self._fire
        if f.station_x == 0.0 and f.station_y == 0.0:
            return f.x, f.y
        return f.station_x, f.station_y

    def _station_alt(self):
        """Bombing height for this drone's slot in the crew.

        Crews are stacked vertically as well as spread over a ring, so this is
        not the same for every member. ~drop_altitude remains the fallback and
        is what member 0 gets.
        """
        f = self._fire
        return f.station_z if f.station_z > 0.0 else self._drop_alt

    def _crew_of(self, hot):
        """Drones on that fire, counting the ones the board has not seen yet.

        A claim published after the board was stamped cannot be in it, so the
        two sources add without double-counting: the board carries every claim
        the spotter had accepted by header.stamp, and _peer_claims carries the
        rest. Claims older than the board are already in `hot.crew` and are
        skipped by the same test, which is also how they expire.
        """
        pending = sum(1 for fire_id, when in self._peer_claims.values()
                      if fire_id == hot.id and when > self._board_stamp)
        return hot.crew + pending

    def _utility(self, hot):
        """Share per second if this drone joined that fire, or None to skip.

        n is crew+1 because the drone is asking what its own arrival would be
        worth, not what the fire is worth now. Both places n appears matter:

          intensity / n     the payout, split equally with everyone who chose
                            it - so joining is a cost.
          work_left / n     the time, because every drone on station does a
                            second of work per second - so joining is also a
                            saving, and on a big fire the saving is bigger.

        Travel is charged at cruise speed and straight-line distance, which is
        optimistic around trees; it does not need to be right, only consistent
        between the fires being compared.

        Two fires are not worth bidding on at all. One whose crew is full, and
        one that is nearly out: `work_left / n` goes to nothing as a job
        finishes, so a fire at 95% is the highest-utility thing on the board by
        a distance, and joining it pays a full share for arriving after it is
        over. Measured: a drone that had bombed a 7 alone for fourteen seconds
        was joined at 0.3 drone-seconds remaining by both others and kept 2.33
        of it. ~join_deadline closes a crew that is already most of the way
        through, and the spotter enforces the same line.
        """
        crew = self._crew_of(hot)
        if crew >= self._max_crew:
            return None
        if crew > 0 and hot.work_total > 0.0:
            if hot.work_done / hot.work_total > self._join_deadline:
                return None
        n = crew + 1
        share = hot.intensity / float(n)
        work_left = max(0.0, hot.work_total - hot.work_done)
        travel = (math.hypot(hot.x - self._x, hot.y - self._y) /
                  max(self._lin_speed, 0.1))
        return share / max(travel + work_left / float(n), 0.1)

    def _choose(self):
        """Best fire on the board, or None if there is nothing to bid on.

        Biggest first, and that is a deliberate choice over the more obvious
        one.

        _utility() below scores share per second, which is the efficient answer:
        it will send a drone to a 3 next door rather than a 9 across the arena,
        because the 3 pays less but pays it much sooner. Efficient, and wrong
        for this scenario. A fire's intensity is what it is worth *and* how big
        it burns, so the 9 is the one that matters and leaving it to burn while
        the fleet clears cheap fires nearby is exactly the behaviour the
        scoring is supposed to discourage.

        So intensity is the primary key and share-per-second only breaks ties
        between fires of equal size. The gates in _utility() still apply and
        still return None - a full crew, or a fire so nearly out that joining
        pays a full share for arriving after the work is done - so "biggest
        first" fills the largest fire to ~max_crew and then moves to the next
        largest, which is what "bigger fires may be worked by more drones"
        means in practice.

        ~value_first false restores the pure efficiency ordering.
        """
        best = None
        best_key = None
        for hot in self._board:
            u = self._utility(hot)
            if u is None:
                continue
            key = (hot.intensity, u) if self._value_first else (0.0, u)
            if best_key is None or key > best_key:
                best, best_key = hot, key
        return best, (best_key[1] if best_key else 0.0)

    def _market_loop(self, _event):
        """Pick a fire, claim it, and keep claiming until it is confirmed.

        Only ever runs when the drone owes nobody anything. The one thing it
        does while committed is notice that it has *stopped* being committed:
        taskings arrive at the spotter's tick rate, so silence means the
        commitment was dropped at the other end - the only way that happens is
        the spotter's not-turning-up timeout, and the drone would otherwise
        fly at a fire nobody thinks is its any more.
        """
        if not self._odom_ready or self._state == 'TAKEOFF':
            return
        # Not while off getting water. The excursion keeps whatever fire this
        # drone holds, so there is nothing to decide, and bidding for a *new*
        # fire with an empty tank is how a drone ends up committed to a job it
        # then has to leave immediately to go and do this.
        if self._state in ('TOWATER', 'REFILL'):
            return
        if self._low() and self._fire is None:
            return
        now = rospy.Time.now()

        if self._fire is not None:
            if (now - self._last_target).to_sec() > self._target_to:
                rospy.logwarn('[%s] no tasking for %.1f s - assuming fire %d '
                              'was taken back', self._ns, self._target_to,
                              self._fire.id)
                self._fire = None
                self._pending = None
                self._state = 'CLIMB'
            return

        # A claim can be refused - the crew filled up while the board was in
        # flight - and nothing answers it directly. Re-sending covers that and
        # a lost message with the same code; re-picking after the timeout
        # covers the case where the refusal is permanent.
        if (self._pending is not None and
                (now - self._pending_since).to_sec() < self._claim_to):
            self._send_claim(self._pending)
            return

        if not self._board:
            return

        hot, utility = self._choose()
        if hot is None:
            return

        self._pending = hot.id
        self._pending_since = now
        self._send_claim(hot.id)
        print('  [%s] %s[BID]%s fire %-2d worth %s%.0f%s, crew %d -> share '
              '%.2f, %.1f drone-s left  (%.2f/s)'
              % (self._ns, _CYAN, _RESET, hot.id, _YELLOW, hot.intensity,
                 _RESET, hot.crew, hot.intensity / float(hot.crew + 1),
                 max(0.0, hot.work_total - hot.work_done), utility))

    def _send_claim(self, fire_id):
        claim = FireClaim()
        claim.drone = self._ns
        claim.fire_id = fire_id
        claim.release = False
        self._claim_pub.publish(claim)

    # ----------------------------------------------------------- display ---
    def _print_header(self):
        print(flush=True)
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET, flush=True)
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '|   UAV FIRE SUPPRESSOR  -  %-22s |' % self._ns + _RESET, flush=True)
        print(_BOLD + _WHITE + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET, flush=True)
        print('  %scruise_alt%s   = %.2f m     %sdrop_altitude%s = %.2f m'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._cruise_alt, _CYAN, _RESET, self._drop_alt), flush=True)
        print('  %sarrive_rad%s   = %.2f m     %slinear_speed%s  = %.2f m/s'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._arrive_rad, _CYAN, _RESET, self._lin_speed), flush=True)
        print('  %savoid_dist%s   = %.2f m     %sstop_dist%s     = %.2f m'
              % (_CYAN, _RESET, self._avoid_dist, _CYAN, _RESET, self._stop_dist), flush=True)
        print(flush=True)

    def _print_drop(self, drop, fire):
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET, flush=True)
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '|  *** FIRE SUPPRESSED  %-6s  (job #%4d) ***   |'
              % (self._ns, drop) + _RESET, flush=True)
        print(_BOLD + _GREEN + '+--------------------------------------------------+' + _RESET, flush=True)
        print('  Fire value       : %s%s%5.0f%s  [%s]'
              % (_YELLOW, _BOLD, fire.intensity, _RESET, _bar(fire.intensity)))
        print('  Crew             : %d drone(s), %.1f drone-s of work'
              % (fire.crew, fire.work_total), flush=True)
        print('  This drone\'s cut : %s%s%5.2f%s  (%.0f / %d)'
              % (_YELLOW, _BOLD, fire.reward, _RESET,
                 fire.intensity, max(fire.crew, 1)))
        print('  Total score      : %s%s%7.2f%s' % (_YELLOW, _BOLD, self._score, _RESET), flush=True)
        print('  Fire (id %-3d)    : (%6.2f, %6.2f) on the ground'
              % (fire.id, fire.x, fire.y), flush=True)
        print('  Dropped from     : (%6.2f, %6.2f, %5.2f) m'
              % (self._x, self._y, self._z), flush=True)
        print(flush=True)
        if len(self._history) > 1:
            print('  Recent jobs:', flush=True)
            for d, v, t in self._history[-5:]:
                marker = _GREEN + '>>>' + _RESET if d == drop else '   '
                print('    %s Job #%-3d +%-5.2f => total %.2f' % (marker, d, v, t), flush=True)
            print(flush=True)

    def _status_display(self, _event):
        if not self._odom_ready:
            return
        state_col = {'ALIGN': _CYAN, 'CHASE': _GREEN, 'DESCEND': _YELLOW,
                     'CLIMB': _CYAN, 'WAIT': _YELLOW, 'TAKEOFF': _WHITE,
                     'TOWATER': _BLUE, 'REFILL': _BLUE}.get(self._state, _WHITE)

        water = '%s%3.0f L%s' % (_BLUE if self._low() else _CYAN,
                                 self._litres, _RESET)

        if self._fire is None:
            print('  [%s] [%s%-7s%s] pos=(%6.2f,%6.2f,%5.2f) water=%s %s '
                  'score=%s%s%7.2f%s jobs=%d'
                  % (self._ns, state_col, self._state, _RESET,
                     self._x, self._y, self._z, water,
                     ('at %s' % self._tender) if self._tender else
                     ('bidding on fire %d' % self._pending if self._pending
                      is not None else 'unassigned'),
                     _YELLOW, _BOLD, self._score, _RESET, self._drops))
            return

        f = self._fire
        tx, ty = self._station()
        dist_xy = math.hypot(tx - self._x, ty - self._y)
        avoid_tag = ' %s[AVOID]%s' % (_RED, _RESET) if self._avoiding else ''
        # The fence replaces the target, so say so - otherwise the status line
        # shows a fire the drone has temporarily stopped flying toward.
        if self._fenced:
            avoid_tag += ' %s[FENCE]%s' % (_RED, _RESET)
        done = (100.0 * f.work_done / f.work_total) if f.work_total > 0 else 0.0
        print('  [%s] [%s%-7s%s]%s pos=(%6.2f,%6.2f,%5.2f) '
              'fire%-2d=(%5.2f,%5.2f) dist=%s%5.2f%s crew=%d cut=%.2f '
              'work=%3.0f%% water=%s score=%s%s%7.2f%s jobs=%d'
              % (self._ns, state_col, self._state, _RESET, avoid_tag,
                 self._x, self._y, self._z, f.id, f.x, f.y,
                 _CYAN, dist_xy, _RESET, f.crew, f.share, done, water,
                 _YELLOW, _BOLD, self._score, _RESET, self._drops), flush=True)

    # --------------------------------------------------------- callbacks ---
    def _odom_cb(self, msg):
        self._x = msg.pose.pose.position.x
        self._y = msg.pose.pose.position.y
        self._z = msg.pose.pose.position.z
        q = msg.pose.pose.orientation
        self._roll, self._pitch, self._yaw = euler_from_quaternion(
            [q.x, q.y, q.z, q.w])
        self._odom_ready = True

    def _scan_cb(self, msg):
        self._scan = msg

    def _board_cb(self, msg):
        self._board = msg.hotspots
        self._board_stamp = msg.header.stamp

    def _water_cb(self, msg):
        self._tenders = [t for t in msg.tanks if t.tender]
        for tank in msg.tanks:
            if tank.vehicle == self._ns:
                self._litres = tank.litres
                self._capacity = tank.capacity
                break

    # ------------------------------------------------------------- water ---
    def _thirsty(self):
        """Out of water, and therefore achieving nothing over a fire."""
        return self._litres <= self._dry

    def _low(self):
        """Not worth starting a new job on."""
        return self._litres < self._reserve

    def _full(self):
        return self._litres >= self._full_frac * self._capacity

    def _pick_tender(self):
        """The tender to fly to: nearest one standing, else nearest with water.

        The first version of this would only accept a tender that was *ready* -
        stopped, and therefore safe to descend onto - and would hold position
        if none were. That reasoning is right about the descent and wrong about
        the transit, and the difference cost two drones a whole run.

        What happened: the tenders are driving much of the time, and rarely
        both at once. So on one tick scout_0 was ready and scout_1 was not, on
        the next it was the other way round, and the drone re-picked every
        tick. Its destination flipped between two vehicles on opposite sides of
        the arena, the heading error never fell below the threshold that lets
        it fly forward, and it sat at (-1.29, 2.15) yawing back and forth with
        an empty tank while a tender stood 3 m away. Measured: both uav_0 and
        uav_1 stuck like that, 0 L, indefinitely.

        So readiness is checked where it actually matters - at the descent, in
        _water_run - and not here. A tender in transit is usually seconds from
        standing, the drone is seconds from arriving, and flying towards it in
        the meantime is exactly right. Ready ones are still preferred; this
        only stops "none ready this instant" from meaning "go nowhere".
        """
        ready, ready_d = None, float('inf')
        any_tender, any_d = None, float('inf')
        for tank in self._tenders:
            if tank.litres <= 0.1:
                continue
            d = math.hypot(tank.x - self._x, tank.y - self._y)
            if tank.ready and d < ready_d:
                ready, ready_d = tank, d
            if d < any_d:
                any_tender, any_d = tank, d
        return ready if ready is not None else any_tender

    def _tender_now(self):
        """Fresh position for the tender this drone is working with."""
        if self._tender is None:
            return None
        for tank in self._tenders:
            if tank.vehicle == self._tender:
                return tank
        return None

    def _station_on(self, cmd, tx, ty, tz):
        """Hold a world point at a given altitude. Body-frame, like DESCEND.

        Factored out because the water excursion needs it three times over -
        holding the ring, following a tender that is still driving, and sitting
        on the deck taking water - and all three are the same manoeuvre with a
        different target.
        """
        dx, dy = tx - self._x, ty - self._y
        cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (tz - self._z),
                              -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
        bx = dx * math.cos(self._yaw) + dy * math.sin(self._yaw)
        by = -dx * math.sin(self._yaw) + dy * math.cos(self._yaw)
        cmd.linear.x = _clamp(self._stn_gain * bx,
                              -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
        cmd.linear.y = _clamp(self._stn_gain * by,
                              -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
        return cmd

    def _hold_alt(self):
        """Transit and holding altitude for this drone's slot.

        The ring separates where three drones *stop*; it does nothing about how
        they get there. A drone flying to its slot takes a straight line that
        can pass directly over the tender - and so over whoever is descending
        onto it. Measured after the ring went in: 0.43 m between one aircraft
        in REFILL and another in TOWATER, which is inside rotor contact.

        So the whole water excursion is stacked as well as spread, the same
        answer a fire's crew uses. Slot 0 transits at cruise, slot 1 at 0.40 m
        above it, slot 2 at 0.80 m - which is more than a rotor span, so two
        drones converging on one tender cannot reach each other whatever their
        paths do.
        """
        return self._cruise_alt + self._hold_step * self._hold_i

    def _hold_point(self, tank):
        """Where this drone waits its turn for a tender, at cruise altitude.

        A tender is a single point and three drones want it, so without this
        they all fly to that point and station-keep on it. Measured on the
        first run: 0.20 m between two aircraft over one tender, one climbing
        away full while the next descended into the same column, with rotor
        tips 0.396 m out. A fire solved this with numbered crew stations; a
        tender is the same problem with a queue rather than a crew.

        The bearing is a numbered slot from ~hold_index of ~hold_count, the
        same answer the scouts' docking ring uses, and it is a launch parameter
        rather than anything derived. Deriving it from the drone's own name was
        tried first and is a trap: uav_0, uav_1 and uav_2 differ by one
        character, so hashing them put the three holding points 115, 116 and
        117 degrees round the ring - 3 cm apart, which is worse than not
        separating them at all. Three drones on a 2.0 m ring at proper spacing
        sit 3.46 m apart.
        """
        if self._hold_n <= 1:
            theta = 0.0
        else:
            theta = 2.0 * math.pi * self._hold_i / float(self._hold_n)
        return (tank.x + self._hold_rad * math.cos(theta),
                tank.y + self._hold_rad * math.sin(theta))

    def _start_refill(self, why):
        """Break off for water, keeping whatever fire this drone holds."""
        tender = self._pick_tender()
        if tender is None:
            rospy.logwarn_throttle(
                5.0, '[%s] %s but no tender is standing ready - holding',
                self._ns, why)
            return False
        self._tender = tender.vehicle
        self._state = 'TOWATER'
        print('  [%s] %s[DRY]%s %s - going to %s at (%.2f, %.2f)%s'
              % (self._ns, _BLUE, _RESET, why, tender.vehicle, tender.x,
                 tender.y,
                 ', still holding fire %d' % self._fire.id
                 if self._fire is not None else ''), flush=True)
        return True

    def _water_run(self, cmd):
        """Fly to a tender and take water. Returns the command to publish.

        Kept apart from the fire-flying states rather than folded into them,
        because the destination is a *vehicle* rather than a place: it can
        drive off, it can empty, and it can stop being ready while this
        aircraft is still on the way. A fire never does any of that, so none of
        the fire states have a reason to check.
        """
        tank = self._tender_now()
        if tank is None or tank.litres <= 0.05:
            # Only re-pick when the chosen tender is genuinely no use - gone
            # from the board, or empty. Emphatically *not* merely because it
            # started moving: see _pick_tender() for the run that cost.
            self._tender = None
            if self._start_refill('tender is dry'):
                tank = self._tender_now()
            if tank is None:
                cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._hold_alt() - self._z),
                                      -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
                return cmd

        hx, hy = self._hold_point(tank)
        my_turn = (tank.serving == self._ns)

        if self._full():
            # Full, but still sitting in the tender's column at 0.95 m, and the
            # next drone in the queue is about to be told to come down into it.
            # Clear out sideways to the holding point *before* handing the slot
            # on. Skipping this was measured at 0.20 m between one aircraft
            # climbing away and the next descending.
            clear = (math.hypot(hx - self._x, hy - self._y) < 0.6 and
                     self._z > self._hold_alt() - 0.35)
            if not clear:
                return self._station_on(cmd, hx, hy, self._hold_alt())
            print('  [%s] %s[FULL]%s %.0f L from %s - back to work'
                  % (self._ns, _BLUE, _RESET, self._litres, tank.vehicle),
                  flush=True)
            self._tender = None
            self._state = 'CLIMB'
            cmd.linear.z = self._vert_speed
            return cmd

        # Where this drone is trying to be: the tender itself once it has been
        # nominated, its slot on the holding ring until then. Getting this
        # wrong is a deadlock rather than a wobble - the first version always
        # flew at the holding point and only entered REFILL from within
        # refill_radius of the tender, so a drone that reached its slot sat
        # there being nominated and never closed the last two metres. Measured:
        # both tenders standing full, given=0 L, two drones dry.
        tx, ty = (tank.x, tank.y) if my_turn else (hx, hy)
        dx = tx - self._x
        dy = ty - self._y
        dist = math.hypot(dx, dy)

        # REFILL: over the deck, hold it and come down into the band. The
        # geometry is the same station-keeping DESCEND uses over a fire, and it
        # is safe for the same reason - the thing underneath is stationary.
        #
        # Entered only when this drone is the one the tender has nominated. A
        # tender is a single point, so the queue is what keeps three aircraft
        # from holding it at once; everybody else waits at their own bearing on
        # a ring, at cruise, which is 1.05 m above whoever is being served.
        if my_turn and (self._state == 'REFILL' or dist <= self._refill_rad):
            if self._state != 'REFILL':
                self._state = 'REFILL'
            if dist > 2.0 * self._refill_rad:
                self._state = 'TOWATER'
                cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._hold_alt() - self._z),
                                      -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
                return cmd

            # Overhead, but the tender is still driving. Hold *at cruise* and
            # follow it until it stands. This is where readiness is checked,
            # and it is the only place it needs to be: what it guards against
            # is descending onto a moving vehicle, not flying above one.
            if not tank.ready:
                return self._station_on(cmd, tank.x, tank.y, self._hold_alt())

            return self._station_on(cmd, tank.x, tank.y,
                                    max(self._refill_alt, self._min_alt))

        # Not this drone's turn. Hold the ring at cruise until it is - close
        # enough to be nominated the moment the slot frees, far enough that
        # nothing is above anything else.
        if not my_turn:
            if self._state == 'REFILL':
                self._state = 'TOWATER'
            if dist <= self._refill_rad:
                return self._station_on(cmd, hx, hy, self._hold_alt())

        # TOWATER: transit, at cruise, exactly like CHASE - body-frame forward
        # with proportional yaw, filtered by the laser. The tender is on the
        # ground among the same trees the fires are.
        cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._hold_alt() - self._z),
                              -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
        angle_err = _normalize_angle(math.atan2(dy, dx) - self._yaw)

        # Yaw on the spot only when badly off heading AND not in the middle of
        # a deflection - exactly the rule CHASE uses, and it has to be here for
        # exactly the same reason. Without the avoid_until half of it this
        # branch re-aimed at the tender every tick: the filter turned the
        # aircraft off a trunk, the heading error to the tender went large, the
        # yaw-only path took over and turned it straight back, and it made no
        # forward progress at all. Measured on the first Gazebo run of this
        # package - uav_1 parked at (3.55, -3.20) with [AVOID] latched on and
        # an empty tank, 1.26 m from the tree it could not get past.
        if (abs(angle_err) > 2.0 * self._angle_tol and
                rospy.Time.now() >= self._avoid_until):
            cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                                   -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)
            return cmd

        speed_factor = (max(0.3, dist / self._slow_dist)
                        if dist < self._slow_dist else 1.0)
        cmd.linear.x = self._lin_speed * speed_factor
        cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                               -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)
        return self._safety_filter(cmd)

    def _peer_pose_cb(self, msg, peer):
        p = msg.pose.pose.position
        self._peer_pose[peer] = (p.x, p.y, p.z)

    def _peer_claim_cb(self, msg):
        """Somebody else's choice, heard on the shared claim topic.

        The board is a tenth of a second old and the whole fleet decides at the
        same time, so without this a drone is bidding against a picture in
        which nobody else has moved. Measured on the first run of this: the
        three aircraft chose within 30 ms of each other, every one of them read
        crew=0, and all three went to the same fire every single time - twice
        onto fires small enough that a crew of three was the worst available
        option for all of them. Nothing was wrong with the arithmetic; it was
        being fed a board that had not heard the other two yet.
        """
        if msg.drone == self._ns:
            return
        if msg.release:
            self._peer_claims.pop(msg.drone, None)
            return
        self._peer_claims[msg.drone] = (msg.fire_id, rospy.Time.now())

    def _target_cb(self, msg):
        """The spotter confirming this drone's job, or closing it out.

        This arrives at the spotter's tick rate for as long as the commitment
        stands, which makes it two things at once: the current station, crew
        size and work counter, and the heartbeat that _market_loop reads as
        proof the drone still holds the fire.

        Most of these change nothing. The message that does is the first one
        for a fire the drone was not already working - a claim being accepted -
        and the response is ALIGN, because the heading it was flying belongs to
        wherever it was before.

        States that ignore even that: DESCEND, because the station moving under
        a crew change is a station-keeping problem rather than a reason to
        stop bombing and yaw; and CLIMB/TAKEOFF, which have to finish getting
        the aircraft to a sane altitude first and hand over to ALIGN
        themselves.
        """
        prev = self._fire
        self._last_target = rospy.Time.now()

        if msg.suppressed:
            # The fire is out and the value has been split. reward is this
            # drone's share, not the fire's value: on a crew of three a 9 pays
            # 3 here and 3 to each of the others.
            self._score += msg.reward
            self._drops += 1
            self._history.append((self._drops, msg.reward, self._score))
            self._print_drop(self._drops, msg)
            self._fire = None
            self._pending = None
            self._state = 'CLIMB'
            return

        self._fire = msg
        if self._pending == msg.id:
            self._pending = None

        if prev is not None and prev.id == msg.id:
            return
        if self._state in ('WAIT', 'TAKEOFF', 'DESCEND', 'CLIMB'):
            return

        print('  [%s] %s[ON FIRE %d]%s at (%.2f, %.2f) worth %s%.0f%s, crew of '
              '%d -> %.2f each, %.1f drone-s left'
              % (self._ns, _CYAN, msg.id, _RESET, msg.x, msg.y,
                 _YELLOW, msg.intensity, _RESET, msg.crew, msg.share,
                 max(0.0, msg.work_total - msg.work_done)))
        self._state = 'ALIGN'

    # -------------------------------------------------------- control loop --
    def _control_loop(self, _event):
        if not self._odom_ready:
            return

        cmd = Twist()

        # Lost aircraft first, before any state does anything with it. There
        # is no command that helps here - see "Geofence and rescue" above.
        # Except just after a respawn, when the attitude being read is a drone
        # bouncing on its landing gear rather than one in trouble.
        if rospy.Time.now() < self._settle_until:
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        if self._lost():
            self._rescue()
            return

        # TAKEOFF ---------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'TAKEOFF':
            error = self._cruise_alt - self._z
            if abs(error) < 0.15:
                self._state = 'WAIT'
                self._wait_ticks = 2
                rospy.loginfo('[%s] airborne at %.2f m - on task', self._ns, self._z)
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * error, -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # WATER -----------------------------------------------------------
        # Checked before anything to do with fires, and deliberately before
        # the no-tasking early-out below, because a refill excursion is
        # orthogonal to whether this drone currently holds a job. It can be dry
        # with a fire - a detour, and the claim is kept - or low without one,
        # in which case it tops up before bidding rather than arriving at
        # somebody's fire with two seconds of water.
        if self._state in ('TOWATER', 'REFILL'):
            self._cmd_pub.publish(self._water_run(cmd))
            return

        if self._thirsty() or (self._fire is None and self._low()):
            if self._start_refill('tank empty' if self._thirsty()
                                  else 'below reserve'):
                self._cmd_pub.publish(self._water_run(cmd))
                return
            # No tender standing anywhere. Fall through and keep flying: a
            # drone with a little water left is still worth something over a
            # fire, and one with none is at least in position for when a
            # tender does stand.

        if self._fire is None:
            # Airborne with no tasking yet, or between a drop and the next
            # assignment. Hold cruise altitude rather than publishing an empty
            # Twist, which commands zero velocity and lets the drone sink.
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._cruise_alt - self._z),
                                  -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # CLIMB -----------------------------------------------------------
        # Get back up before flying anywhere. Checked before the geofence and
        # before any horizontal command is built, because the whole point is
        # that nothing else happens until the aircraft has its height back.
        if self._state == 'CLIMB':
            error = self._cruise_alt - self._z
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * error, -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)
            if abs(error) <= self._climb_tol:
                self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # Soft geofence. Outside it, the middle of the arena becomes the
        # target and the fire waits - a drone drifting toward the wall it is
        # about to be upset by should not still be working a fire.
        if self._outside_fence():
            tx, ty = 0.0, 0.0
            self._fenced = True
            rospy.logwarn_throttle(
                5.0, '[%s] outside the fence at (%.1f, %.1f) - returning'
                % (self._ns, self._x, self._y))
        else:
            self._fenced = False
            tx, ty = self._station()

        dx = tx - self._x
        dy = ty - self._y
        dist_xy = math.hypot(dx, dy)

        # Altitude is driven in every flying state, because it is independent
        # of heading and there is no reason to give up height while turning.
        # Cruise everywhere except DESCEND, which overrides this below - the
        # target is on the ground now, so there is no per-target altitude to
        # track on the way there the way there was with floating treasure.
        cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (self._cruise_alt - self._z),
                              -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)

        # WAIT ------------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'WAIT':
            self._wait_ticks -= 1
            if self._wait_ticks <= 0:
                self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        # DESCEND ---------------------------------------------------------
        # On station over the fire: hold it and come down. Entered from CHASE,
        # and left only by the spotter confirming the fire is out (which sends
        # this node to CLIMB) or by the drone drifting back outside the arrival
        # radius.
        #
        # This is where the work is done and it is no longer brief. A fire is
        # put out by drone-seconds, so a 10 with nobody else on it holds the
        # aircraft here for twelve of them - which is the cost the market
        # arithmetic in _utility is weighing a smaller share against.
        if self._state == 'DESCEND' or (self._state == 'CHASE' and
                                        not self._fenced and
                                        dist_xy <= self._arrive_rad):
            if self._state != 'DESCEND':
                self._state = 'DESCEND'
            if dist_xy > 2.0 * self._arrive_rad:
                # Blown off the fire. Climbing first rather than chasing at
                # drop height keeps the transit rules honest.
                self._state = 'CLIMB'
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

            target_z = max(self._station_alt(), self._min_alt)
            cmd.linear.z = _clamp(1.0 * (target_z - self._z),
                                  -self._vert_speed, self._vert_speed)

            # Station-keeping, in the body frame. The world-frame error is
            # rotated by yaw because /cmd_vel is body-stabilized; this is the
            # one phase that commands lateral velocity, and it is safe here
            # only because the drone is stopped over an assigned fire that the
            # spotter placed clear of the trees.
            bx = dx * math.cos(self._yaw) + dy * math.sin(self._yaw)
            by = -dx * math.sin(self._yaw) + dy * math.cos(self._yaw)
            cmd.linear.x = _clamp(self._stn_gain * bx,
                                  -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
            cmd.linear.y = _clamp(self._stn_gain * by,
                                  -self._stn_speed, self._stn_speed)
            # Lateral only, not the full three-sector filter. The whole filter
            # would back the aircraft off and yaw it away from the very fire it
            # is descending onto - the fire IS the goal here, and something
            # 1.5 m ahead at station altitude is usually the crew ring or the
            # tender, not an obstruction. What does need shaping is the strafe:
            # this is the one command in the node that moves the aircraft
            # sideways, and it was previously published raw.
            self._cmd_pub.publish(self._peer_filter(self._lateral_filter(cmd)))
            return

        target_angle = math.atan2(dy, dx)
        angle_err = _normalize_angle(target_angle - self._yaw)

        # ALIGN -----------------------------------------------------------
        if self._state == 'ALIGN':
            if abs(angle_err) <= self._angle_tol:
                self._state = 'CHASE'
            else:
                cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                                       -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)
                self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
                return

        # CHASE -----------------------------------------------------------
        # Drop back to ALIGN if the heading has drifted badly - but not while
        # avoiding, and not for avoid_hold afterwards. The safety filter yaws
        # deliberately off-target, and tb3_treasure's unconditional version of
        # this check would read that as drift, hand control to ALIGN, and turn
        # straight back into the pillar the filter had just steered around.
        #
        # Suppressing it only while the filter is *currently* triggered is not
        # enough, and the reason is that clearing the front sector is the one
        # thing a successful deflection guarantees. The drone turns off the
        # pillar, the sector clears, this check fires on the same tick, ALIGN
        # takes over and yaws it straight back, and the pillar is in front
        # again - now with no forward motion to show for it, because ALIGN does
        # not translate. That is a livelock, not a wobble: a drone 2.5 m from
        # its target ping-ponged ALIGN/CHASE for the last 90 seconds of a run
        # and finished with two collections against another drone's twelve.
        # The hold buys enough committed forward flight - about 0.9 m at cruise
        # for every second of it - to actually pass the thing, which is a
        # slightly longer lateral offset than a tree plus a rotor span.
        if (abs(angle_err) > 2.0 * self._angle_tol and
                rospy.Time.now() >= self._avoid_until):
            self._state = 'ALIGN'
            self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)
            return

        speed_factor = (max(0.3, dist_xy / self._slow_dist)
                        if dist_xy < self._slow_dist else 1.0)

        # Body-frame forward. hector rotates this by the current yaw itself -
        # see "Which frame /cmd_vel is in" at the top of this file - so flying
        # along the heading is what commanding x alone already means.
        cmd.linear.x = self._lin_speed * speed_factor
        cmd.linear.y = 0.0
        cmd.angular.z = _clamp(self._ang_gain * angle_err,
                               -self._max_ang, self._max_ang)

        cmd = self._safety_filter(cmd)

        self._cmd_pub.publish(cmd)


def main():
    FireSuppressor()
    rospy.spin()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
